>> title: Cream Cheese
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: cultured, fresh cheese, no-cook, spread, make-ahead
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: farmer style cream cheese, soft cheese, schmear base, fresh cream cheese, cheese spread
>> pairs-with: bagels, scallion-schmear, new-york-cheesecake
>> servings: 12
>> time: 1 day 1 hr 30 min
>> slack: narrow — above about 85°F the culture races and the curd goes sour and grainy instead of clean and sweet, and a grainy curd beats out grainy; the twelve-hour drain is forgiving in the other direction, since a longer hang only gives a firmer cheese
>> step.1: warm the milk and cream to 72°F (22°C)
>> step.2: stir in the culture, ferment 12 hr
>> step.3: set with rennet 1 hr
>> step.4: drain in cloth 12 hr
>> step.5: beat with salt

Warm @whole milk{4%cups}(950 mL) and @heavy cream{2%cups}(475 mL) together to 72°F (22°C), which is blood-warm to the back of a finger and no warmer. Above about 85°F the culture races and the curd goes sour and grainy instead of clean and sweet.

Stir @cultured buttermilk{1/4%cup}(60 mL; live, check the label) into @&(~1)warm milk{}, cover it, and ~ferment{12%hr} on the counter until it has thickened to the texture of pourable yogurt and smells faintly of sour cream. That is the whole flavour of the finished cheese, arriving before any cheese exists.

Set @&(~1)cultured milk{} with @liquid rennet{1%drop}(diluted in 2 Tbs of cool water) stirred in for exactly twenty seconds, then leave it undisturbed to ~set{1%hr}. One drop, not two: cream cheese wants the barest set, and more rennet gives you something closer to a rubbery paneer.

Ladle @&(~1)curd{} into a doubled cloth over a bowl, tie it, and ~drain{12%hr} in the fridge until the dripping has all but stopped. Squeezing hurries it and costs you the texture; hanging it in the cold does the same job while you sleep.

Beat @&(~1)drained curd{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp} until it turns glossy and takes a swirl. It is looser than the foil-wrapped brick — that one is stabilised with gums — and it keeps ten days. This is the base under the schmear, and it is what the cheesecake wants too.
